  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1\n\nSome partial clearing will work its way west to east this evening. \nThen partly cloudy conditions prevail overnight, with some patchy \nfog possible from the Twin Tiers south into NE PA. Late tonight into \nTuesday morning the warm front will begin pushing north-northeast \nover the area. This may initiate some showers and isolated elevated \nthunderstorms, mainly along and north of the I-90 corridor. A few of \nthis isolated showers could clip the Twin Tiers Tuesday morning, \notherwise it will be partly sunny from the NY Southern Tier south \ninto NE PA Tuesday morning. Eventually the warm front should lift \nnortheast, clearing the CWA by midday or early Tuesday afternoon. \nBehind this feature, expect mostly sunny skies once again from the \nTwin Tiers south. Further north, near I-90 the clouds linger much of \nthe day with additional shower and thunderstorm chances expected. \nThere is a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5 ) for an isolated strong or \nsevere storm across northern areas, with the greatest potential \nbeing for isolated damaging wind gusts. Instability will reach close \nto 1000 J/kg of MLCAPE and bulk shear should be close to 40 kts. \nOtherwise, Tuesday will feature very warm temperatures and humid \nconditions. Highs will reach well into the 70s (and even low 80s in \nthe deeper valley locations).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2\n\nA moist west-northwest flow takes hold over the area on Wednesday, \nas the nearly stalled cold front lingers over south-central PA. \nModel guidance is showing the potential for a few light showers or \nsprinkles traversing the area during the afternoon hours. Otherwise, \nWednesday will be partly sunny to mostly cloudy with highs in the \n60s to around 70 for CNY, and low to mid-70s in NE PA. \n\nA much drier air mass and stronger surface high pressure center \nfinally reaches the area for Thursday. This should bring plenty of \nsunshine, lower humidity and comfortable temperatures in the upper \n50s to mid-60s. Northwest winds could be a little breezy Wednesday \nnight into Thursday morning behind the departing front.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3\n\nOur next impactful system looks to be Thursday night through Friday, \nfollowed by a deep upper trough settling over the Northeast U.S.\ninto Saturday. Widespread rain showers will be possible again \nThursday night through Friday as a fast moving low pressure system \ncrosses the region. This system will be followed by a deep upper \ntrough/low diving into the area from central Canada.\n\nThis trough has been modeled consistently between most of the major \nglobal models, so confidence continues to increase for unsettled \nconditions this Saturday. T850 could dip as low as -8C over Central \nNY on Saturday as cold air advection takes hold on NW flow. Lake \nAmerica water temperatures are quite warm, ranging in the upper 50s \nto low 60s. Such a large  difference in air and water temperatures \nshould produce a  decent lake response and our first lake effect \nrain and snow event of  the season is possible if this forecast \npattern takes shape. The lake effect precipitation looks to taper \noff fairly quickly by later Saturday as high pressure moves in.\n\nSome very light elevation dependent wet snow accumulation could \noccur, but there remains a lot of uncertainty in just hold cold \nboundary layer temperatures will be. Current data shows highs in the \n40s Saturday afternoon, so snow accumulations will be difficult and \nunlikely during the day. \n\nHigh pressure returns for Sunday and Monday next week. This will \nbring sunny skies and a return to seasonable temperatures during the \nday time. Overnight lows will be cold Saturday night in the mid-20s \nto low 30s areawide, bringing the first widespread freeze to some \nlocations.",
